edit(a,2,steps=['Under constant churn per billing period, expected active billed periods are approximately 1/churn, including the first active period.','At 25% monthly churn this is four monthly periods; multiply by orders per active period to estimate orders.','An annual renewal model with 40% annual churn gives 2.5 annual billed periods; it is not identical to a monthly cancellation model.','Compare plans using their actual billing and cancellation schedules and observed cohort revenue.','Treat lower churn as a scenario to validate, not guaranteed permission to increase CAC.'])
edit(a,10,title='Convert monthly and annual churn on a consistent survival basis',summary='Normalize subscription churn through retained-customer survival before comparing rates.',steps=['For constant monthly churn m, annual churn = 1 - (1-m)^12.','For annual churn a, equivalent constant monthly churn = 1 - (1-a)^(1/12).','A 40% annual churn rate corresponds to about 4.17% monthly churn under this assumption.','Use actual billing periods for lifetime revenue and payback: annual upfront subscriptions differ from monthly cancel-anytime plans.','Compare equivalent cohorts, observation windows and cancellation definitions.'],caveats=['These conversions assume constant churn and no reactivation; real cohort survival may differ.'])
a='f5902e69464c4c6cb8c5d24d'
edit(a,4,summary='Estimate lifetime subscription orders from billed periods, order frequency and contribution per shipment.',steps=['Estimate churn for the store\'s actual billing period; the source\'s 25% monthly churn is an illustration.','Expected active periods are 1/churn in a constant-churn model. Orders equal active periods times orders per period; four periods mean four orders only with one shipment each period.','Multiply expected orders by contribution per shipment after discounts, fulfillment and returns.','Compare observed subscription and one-time-purchase cohorts, including acquisition cost and cash payback.','Blend cohort values only with matching acquisition cohorts and realistic subscription conversion rates.'])
edit(a,7,title='Model tax losses separately from book losses and cash tax payments',summary='Keep tax-loss assumptions explicit when forecasting cash for a store approaching profitability.',steps=['Separate accounting losses from tax losses in the forecast.','Have the accountant confirm eligible carryforwards, restrictions, applicable rates and payment dates for the entity.','Apply only confirmed usable losses to forecast taxable income; do not assume every operating loss shields later profit.','Do not recognize a deferred tax asset automatically from accumulated losses; confirm the applicable recognition requirements.','Maintain a cash-tax calendar and downside case without uncertain relief.'],caveats=['The source\'s tax rate and eleven-month shelter are historical scenario assumptions, not current rules.'])
r=read(DEST/'results'/f'{a}.json')['cards'][9];s=r['steps'];s[0]='In a simplified bridge, start with net income, add back inventory COGS expensed, then subtract all actual inventory supplier cash payments, whether goods have sold or not.';s[1]='Add other non-cash and working-capital adjustments separately. Do not also subtract inventory/payables movements already captured by the supplier-payment bridge.';edit(a,9,steps=s)
